A||||14||GT Bastard||How the heck do I separate the tie rod end?||||9||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||04-19-2000||04:44 PM||||I'm changing my alternator and the only possible way to gain clearance is to disconnect the tie-rod end link (according to Chiltons.)  I've removed the nut from the top, removed the cotter pin and the nut from the bottom, but how do I disconnect the tie-rod end from the suspension?  I thoguht it would easily come apart, but it's tight as hell.  Any suggestions? ||||reg||9
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||04-19-2000||04:51 PM||||You need a Tie Rod Separater & a hammer.. It looks like a U with a handle under it. You slip the U part above the tie rod end and tap it with a hammer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 04-19-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000002||Fret Naught||04-19-2000||04:55 PM||||If you don't want to rip the boot, then sometimes if you give it a good rap close to the knuckle you won't need a tie rod fork (we call them a pickle fork) but don't beat the snot out of it just a couple of good raps. If it doesn't come loose then you need the fork||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||04-19-2000||05:10 PM||||Curses!  Is this going to be a pain in the arse to re-connect?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||04-19-2000||05:30 PM||||I have always been able to separate tie rod ends using a trick I happened to try about 25 years ago. You need to support the cast iron casting the tie rod goes thru to a solid floor stand. I usually use bricks. But a thick piece of steel will work. Nothing semi soft like a floor jack or wood. It has to support the casting tight with no space under it. Then take a large hammer or small sledge and with the nut removed hit the top of the tie rod end stud sharply. It usually comes in one hit. Some times like on a front end I have to use a long steel rod to get up where I can get a got hit on it. Works every time. Trick is to have a very solid support. <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fret Naught||04-19-2000||05:36 PM||||Nope, Re-connect is a piece of cake, just slide it in and torque it down.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Haze_Performance||04-19-2000||05:37 PM||||Reconnecting is easy. Just torque the nut back to factory specs and it will slip back into place. Good Luck..    ||||reg||7
Z||000007||theogre||04-19-2000||07:14 PM||||If you are reusing the joint don't hit it on the side, or use the fork style splitter.<P>If you whack the bolt turn the nut upside down and tread it even or slightly above the end of the bolt. Other wise you could wreck the bolt.<P>Best option is one of the clamp type seperators. These are very gentle one the joint.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||04-19-2000||07:54 PM||||I've removed lots of outer tie rod ends like Ogre discribed. Remove the cotter pin, unbolt the nut until the top of the nut is even with the top of the threads on the rod, this protects the threads from damage. One good solid rap with a stout hammer usually pops it loose. Don't reinstall it if the rubber boot is damaged. You can get replacement boots from the "Help!" section of auto parts stores in a pinch.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ROB MAC||04-19-2000||07:56 PM||||Theres a tool that hooks on like a fork and you tighten the bolt on it till the tie rod end pops . Cost me 15 canadian bucks . Worked real easy.||||reg||1
Z||000010||GT Bastard||04-19-2000||08:49 PM||||Thanks for all the info guys... I ended up removing the alternator from the top!  I tried smacking (not too hard) like Haze said, and it squirted poop on me!  It's ok though, I didn't damage it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Haze_Performance||04-20-2000||01:06 AM||||Make sure you fill it back up with poop (Tech Term  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">..) before you drive it...  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||pollock||04-20-2000||10:45 AM||||I've replaced my alternator twice and i never had to disconnect the tie rod....  but mine's a V6, is yours a 4?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000013||GT Bastard||04-20-2000||02:41 PM||||Mines a V6 too, but is yours and auto or a manual?  I was short about an inch and a half of clearance when I tried to remove mine out the bottom, and this was with the alternator bracket completely removed.||||reg||1
Z||000014||pollock||04-20-2000||04:27 PM||||manual.  I think i had to remove the pipe that blows air on the alternator.||||reg||1
